3	== Specification/basic/traits.tex:361-605
4	
5	When a method declaration includes a body expression, it is called a
6	\emph{method definition}.
7	A method declaration that does not have its body expression is referred to
8	as an \emph{abstract method declaration}.
9	An abstract method declaration declares an \emph{abstract method};
10	any object inheriting an abstract method must define a body expression for the
11	method.  An abstract method declaration may include the modifier \KWD{abstract}.
12	It may elide parameter names but parameter
13	types cannot be omitted except for the self parameter.
14	
15	
16	\note{
17	Method declarations that are overridden by some declaration are not
18	accessible.  They are not considered for overloading checks.
19	
20	Partial overriding of an overloaded functional is allowed
21	but partial overriding of a functional declaration is not allowed.
22	}
23	
24	A method declaration may have the modifier \KWD{override}.
25	Such a declaration \emph{overrides} any inherited declaration with the same name
26	and the self parameter (if any) at the same position,
27	whose parameter type, not counting the type of the self parameter,
28	is a strict subtype of the overriding declaration's parameter type.
29	The return type of the overriding declaration must be subtype of
30	that of the overridden declaration to preserve type safety.
31	A declaration with the modifier \KWD{override} must override
32	some inherited declaration.
33	It is a static error if a declaration with the modifier \KWD{override}
34	does not override any inherited declaration.
35	
36	Here is an example trait \TYP{Enzyme} which provides methods
37	\VAR{reactionSpeed} and \VAR{catalyze}:
38	\input{\home/basic/examples/Trait.Method.e.tex}
39	\TYP{Enzyme}
40	inherits the abstract method \VAR{mass} from \TYP{OrganicMolecule},
41	declares the abstract method \VAR{reactionSpeed}, and
42	declares the concrete method \VAR{catalyze}
43	which is inherited as an abstract method
44	from its supertrait \TYP{Catalyst}.
